      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}-3y-5$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}+3y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+3y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-3y-5 & =-({(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
