    "q-218663": {
      "dom_id": "step-q218663",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-({z}^{2}+z+2)$ by completing the square.\n\n$-({z}^{2}+z+2)=-(z\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+z+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({z}^{2}+z+2) & =-({(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
